.\" sash.1 - DRAFT man page for every switch-taking surface in safebash.
.\" Every behavior below was verified empirically against
.\" target/release/sash on 2026-09-08, not read off the source comments.
.TH SASH 1 "2026-09-08" "safebash" "General Commands Manual"
.
.SH NAME
sash \- compile a safe subset of bash to a static native binary
.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sash
.RI [ options ]
.I script.sh
.RB [ \-o
.IR output ]
.br
.B sash
.B \-c
.I 'script'
.RB [ \-o
.IR output ]
.br
.B sash
.B \-\-extract
.I binary
.br
.B sash
.B \-\-help
.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sash
compiles a script written in the safe subset of bash to a static ELF
binary. Output is byte-identical to what
.BR bash (1)
produces for the same script.
.PP
This page documents every surface in safebash that takes a switch: the
compiler driver, the three builtins that accept flags
.RB ( read ,
.BR mapfile ,
.BR echo ),
the
.RB \[dq] [[ \ ]] \[dq]
test operators that share the same
.BI \- X
namespace, and
.BR sash\-convert (1).
.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Input and output
.TP
.BI \-c \ script
Compile
.I script
given inline instead of reading a file.
.TP
.BI \-o \ output
Write the binary to
.IR output .
.TP
.B \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the usage summary on standard output and exit 0.
.
.SS Alternative backends
Each writes to standard output and exits; no binary is produced.
.TP
.B \-\-emit\-ir
Emit the IR as an inert Janet data literal \(em data, not a program.
Intended for out-of-tree consumers such as the decoupled oracle
(DD#104); the leading
.B :ir\-version
key lets a consumer refuse a capture it does not understand.
.TP
.B \-\-emit\-c
Emit C source. Link against
.B libsash_rt.a
.RB ( SASH_RT_LIB ).
.TP
.B \-\-manifest
Emit the manifest (external dependencies).
.
.SS Inspection
.TP
.BI \-\-extract \ binary
Dump the
.B .sash_source
ELF section of a compiled
.IR binary .
The binary must follow the flag directly
.RB ( sash \ \-\-extract \ BIN );
it is read positionally.
.TP
.B \-\-trace
Enable tracing in the emitted binary.
Native backend only, and opt\-in twice over: the flag compiles the trace
sites in, and
.B SASH_TRACE=1
activates them at run time. A binary built without it ignores the
variable.
.TP
.BI \-\-heap\-size= size
Set the runtime heap reservation, as a byte count with an optional
.BR K ,
.BR M ,
or
.B G
suffix \(em for example
.BR 512K ,
.BR 64M ,
.BR 2G .
Default 256M (DD#107).
.
The heap is a single fixed region, so this is a hard ceiling rather than a
starting size; exceeding it is a clean
.B sash: out of memory
diagnostic with a non\-zero status, never a segfault. The region is mapped
.BR MAP_NORESERVE ,
so a large reservation costs address space rather than memory.
.
The size is compiled into the binary and is deliberately
.B not
readable from the environment: there is no
.BR $SASH_HEAP .
.
.SS Taint bypasses
See DD#82. These weaken the guarantee that
.B sash
exists to provide; use them deliberately.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-taint\-cst
.B A testing aid only \(em not for general use.
Disable the front-end (CST) taint pass only. The SSA taint backstop still
runs, which is how the backstop is exercised in isolation. Deliberately
absent from
.BR "sash \-\-help" :
it exists to test the compiler, not to be reached for while writing
programs. Use
.B \-\-no\-taint
if you genuinely need a bypass.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-taint
Disable both taint passes.
.
.SS Environment
.TP
.BI \-\-sysroot= path
Set the sysroot used for command resolution (DD#23).
.
.SS Rejected here, accepted by sash\-convert
.B \-\-emit\-source ,
.B \-\-annotate ,
and
.B \-\-nolint
are rejected with a message directing you to
.BR sash\-convert (1).
Source transformation belongs to the converter, not the compiler (DD#57).
.
.SH BUILTINS THAT TAKE FLAGS
.SS read
.B read
.RB [ \-a
.IR array ]
.RB [ \-d
.IR delim ]
.RB [ \-f
.IR sep ]
.RB [ \-r ]
.RI [ name ...]
.PP
A byte-valued flag may be joined
.RB ( \-d: )
or split
.RB ( \-d\ : ).
An empty value
.RB ( \-d\ '' ,
or a bare trailing
.BR \-d )
means NUL, following the bash
.B read -d ''
convention.
.TP
.BI \-a \ array
Read into
.IR array .
See BUGS: the split here uses
.BR \-d ,
not
.BR \-f .
.TP
.BI \-d \ delim
Record delimiter \(em the byte that
.B terminates the line.
Reading stops there. This is
.B not
the field separator.
.TP
.BI \-f \ sep
Field separator \(em the byte that separates fields
.I within
the line. This is bash's
.B IFS
for a single command.
.TP
.B \-r
Accepted and ignored. sash reads are always raw, so
.B \-r
requests the default.
.PP
Any other
.BI \- flag
is rejected with
.BR SB5015 .
.PP
With one target, the whole line is assigned. With several, each target but
the last takes one field and
.B the last takes the remainder:
.PP
.RS
.EX
echo "a b c d" | read x y      # x=a  y="b c d"
echo "a:b:c:d" | read -f: x y  # x=a  y="b:c:d"
echo "a:b:c:d" | read -d: x y  # x=a  y=""      (line ends at the first ':')
.EE
.RE
.PP
.B read
targets are tainted (DD#24); reading one requires
.BR attest .
Use
.B _
to bind and discard a field you do not want (DD#98).
.
.SS mapfile
.B mapfile
.RB [ \-m ]
.RB [ \-f c ]
.RB [ \-t ]
.I target
.TP
.B \-m
Map mode: read
.IB key sep value
lines into a map rather than an array.
.TP
.BI \-f c
The map key/value separator, default
.BR = .
Joined
.RB ( \-f: )
or split
.RB ( \-f\ : ).
.TP
.B \-t
Accepted and ignored. sash always strips the trailing newline.
.PP
.B readarray
is
.B not
supported \(em it is rejected by command resolution
.RB ( SB5011 ).
Use
.BR mapfile .
.PP
Unknown flags are
.B silently ignored
here. See BUGS.
.
.SS echo
.B echo
.RB [ \-n ]
.RI [ arg ...]
.TP
.B \-n
Suppress the trailing newline. Recognized
.B only as the first argument;
elsewhere
.B \-n
is an ordinary word and is printed.
.PP
There is no
.BR \-e .
sash does not interpret backslash escapes in
.BR echo ;
.B \-e
is printed literally, as is the text after it.
.
.SH TEST OPERATORS
The
.RB \[dq] [[ \ ]] \[dq]
operators occupy the same
.BI \- X
namespace as the flags above but are
.B operators, not switches.
This is the most common source of confusion \(em see BUGS.
.SS Unary
.TP
.B \-e \-f \-d \-s \-r \-w \-x \-h \-L \-b \-c \-p \-S \-t \-O \-G \-N \-a
File and descriptor tests, as in
.BR bash (1).
.TP
.B \-n \-z
Non-empty / empty string.
.TP
.B \-v \-o \-R
Parsed but
.B rejected
.RB ( SB5028 ),
each with a hint:
.B \-v
\(em use
.B \-n
instead, since under DD#62 an unset or empty variable is just the empty
string;
.B \-o
\(em sash has no shell options;
.B \-R
\(em sash has no namerefs.
.SS Content shape (DD#93)
.TP
.B i? p? g?
Integer-representable / all-printable / all-graph. These are
.B prefix
operators and carry no dash:
.RB \[dq] [[\ i?\ $x\ ]] \[dq].
They also have an
.B attest
shorthand:
.RB \[dq] attest\ x\ p? \[dq].
The
.B ?
suffix keeps them out of bash's saturated
.BI \- X
namespace.
.SS Numeric comparison
.B \-eq \-ne \-lt \-le \-gt \-ge
are
.B rejected
.RB ( SB5019 ).
Arithmetic comparison belongs in
.RB \[dq] ((\ )) \[dq]:
write
.RB \[dq] ((\ a\ ==\ b\ )) \[dq],
not
.RB \[dq] [[\ $a\ \-eq\ $b\ ]] \[dq].
.
.SH REGULAR EXPRESSIONS
Regexes appear in
.RB \[dq] "[[ $v =~ RE ]]" \[dq],
in the
.BR match ,
.BR sub ,
and
.B gsub
builtins, and in the
.RB \[dq] s/RE/SUB/ \[dq]
sugar.
.PP
The engine is
.BR "Rob Pike's Plan\ 9 regexp" ,
used deliberately. It is a Thompson NFA: no backtracking, so match time is
linear in the subject and no pattern can be made to blow up exponentially.
.B POSIX compliance is not a goal.
A handful of additions have been made for convenience, not for conformance.
Expect a close relative of bash's
.BR =~ ,
not a drop\-in replacement; the differences are listed below and are
intentional.
.SS Core
.TP
.B . * + ? | ( ) ^ $
As in POSIX ERE.
.B .
matches any byte except newline;
.B ^
and
.B $
anchor at the subject's start and end.
.TP
.B [abc] [a\-c] [^abc]
Bracket expressions, with ranges and negation.
.TP
.B \e.
A backslash makes any metacharacter literal.
.SS Additions
These are
.B not
in POSIX ERE and are
.B not
accepted by
.BR bash (1).
.TP
.BI { n } "\fR, \fP" { n , m } "\fR, \fP" { n ,}
Counted repetition of the preceding atom. Each bound is at most
.BR 32 ;
a larger one is rejected
.RB ( "repetition count too large" ).
The open form
.BI { n ,}
is unbounded above.
.TP
.B \ed \eD \ew \eW \es \eS
Digit, word, and whitespace classes, and their complements.
.TP
.BI \ex NN
The byte with hex value
.IR NN ,
exactly two digits. Lets a pattern hold any literal byte \(em a delimiter, a
space, a control character \(em without quoting gymnastics. A non\-hex digit
after
.B \ex
is an error.
.SS Divergences from bash
.TP
.B Matching is byte\-oriented.
.B .
consumes one byte, not one character, so
.RB \[dq] "é =~ ^.$" \[dq]
is false and
.RB \[dq] "é =~ ^..$" \[dq]
is true.
.BR bash (1)
in a UTF\-8 locale matches whole characters. Literal non\-ASCII text in a
pattern still works, because both sides are the same bytes. This matches
.BR strlen ,
which is likewise a byte count.
.TP
.B POSIX character classes are not supported.
.RB \[dq] [[:alpha:]] \[dq]
is read as an ordinary bracket expression followed by the literal text
.RB \[dq] :] \[dq],
so it
.B silently fails to match
rather than being rejected. Use
.B \ew
or an explicit range. This is the one unsupported construct that does not
announce itself.
.TP
.B No backreferences in a pattern.
.B \e1
is the literal character
.BR 1 ,
so
.RB \[dq] "(a)\e1" \[dq]
matches
.RB \[dq] a1 \[dq],
not
.RB \[dq] aa \[dq].
.RB ( sub
and
.B gsub
do take
.B \e0
\-
.B \e9
in the
.IR replacement ;
that is a different position.)
.TP
.B No lookahead, lookbehind, or non\-capturing groups.
.RB \[dq] (?= \[dq],
.RB \[dq] (?<= \[dq],
and
.RB \[dq] (?: \[dq]
are rejected
.RB ( SB5023 ).
.TP
.B No word boundary.
.B \eb
is the literal character
.BR b .
.TP
.B A literal hyphen in a bracket must be escaped.
Write
.RB \[dq] [ab\e\-] \[dq];
.RB \[dq] [ab\-] \[dq]
is rejected as a malformed range.
.TP
.B No counted repetition of a group.
.RB \[dq] (ab){2} \[dq]
is rejected. Write
.RB \[dq] (ab)(ab) \[dq].
.TP
.B No empty alternative.
.RB \[dq] (a|) \[dq]
is rejected;
.BR bash (1)
accepts it. Write
.RB \[dq] (a)? \[dq].
.TP
.B No case\-insensitive matching.
The engine has no
.BR REG_ICASE .
Fold both sides instead:
.RB \[dq] "match $(tolower $x) $(tolower $pat)" \[dq].
.SS Quoting
The pattern is an ordinary argument \(em one word \(em not a specially parsed
field. Quote it whenever it contains characters the shell would otherwise
take: a space, a
.RB \[dq] # \[dq],
or a doubled
.RB \[dq] (( \[dq]
\(em which an unquoted pattern hands to the arithmetic lexer, so
.RB \[dq] "=~ ^((a))$" \[dq]
is rejected with
.RB \[dq] "expected ']]'" \[dq]
while
.RB \[dq] "=~ '^((a))$'" \[dq]
is fine. A single
.RB \[dq] ( \[dq]
needs no quoting.
.RB \[dq] "=~ '^(a|b)$'" \[dq]
is literal,
.RB \[dq] "=~ \[dq]^$p$\[dq]" \[dq]
interpolates, and
.RB \[dq] "=~ $p" \[dq]
takes the whole pattern from a variable. Single quotes are the usual choice,
since a regex is full of characters that mean something to both languages.
.SS Validation
A pattern that is a compile\-time literal is compiled by the same engine
during compilation, so a malformed one is a
.B compile
error
.RB ( SB5023 ).
A pattern that comes from a variable is compiled at run time; a malformed one
then exits with status 2.
.
.SH SASH-CONVERT
.B sash\-convert
.RB [ \-\-annotate ]
.RB [ \-o
.IR output ]
.RI [ input.sh ]
.PP
Converts a bash script to sash-native source. Reads standard input when no
input file is given.
.TP
.B \-\-annotate
Annotate the output.
.TP
.BI \-o \ output
Write to
.IR output .
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage and exit.
.PP
Unlike
.BR sash ,
.B sash\-convert
rejects an unknown flag.
.
.SH BUGS
.SS The \-d / \-f collision
One spelling means three different things depending on where it sits. This
is the confusion this page was written for:
.PP
.RS
.TS
l l l l.
spelling	in read	in mapfile	in [[ ]]
_
\-d	record delimiter	(not a flag)	is a directory
\-f	field separator	key/value separator	is a regular file
\-a	array target	(not a flag)	file exists
\-t	(not a flag)	accepted no-op	is a terminal
\-n	(not a flag)	(not a flag)	string is non-empty
.TE
.RE
.PP
Choosing
.B \-d
when you meant
.B \-f
fails silently \(em the read stops at the first delimiter and the trailing
targets are left empty, with no diagnostic.
.SS read \-a ignores \-f
With
.BR \-a ,
splitting uses the
.B record delimiter
.RB ( \-d ),
and
.B \-f is silently ignored:
.PP
.RS
.EX
echo "a:b:c" | read -a arr -d:   # 3 elements
echo "a:b:c" | read -a arr -f:   # 1 element; -f discarded
.EE
.RE
.PP
The
.B \-d
behavior is deliberate (it is what makes the
.BR sash\-getopt (1)
NUL-splitting idiom work, spec 22.26/22.27). The silent discard of
.B \-f
is not spec'd. bash splits an array read on
.BR IFS ,
i.e. on the field separator.
.SS Standalone and pipeline read disagree
.B read
is lowered by two different paths, and they do not enforce the same rules:
.PP
.RS
.EX
read -f: x                 # SB5016 (field separator needs several targets)
echo "a:b" | read -f: x    # accepted; x gets the whole line
.EE
.RE
.PP
The same divergence affects the DD#86 acknowledgment
.RB \[dq] :\ $v \[dq],
which is accepted after a pipeline
.B read
but rejected after a standalone one \(em and rejected by the SSA taint
backstop, with no
.B SB
code at all. See
.BR notes/tasks/read\-taint\-backstop\-no\-sb\-code.md .
.SS Unknown flags are handled two different ways
.RS
.TP
.B read
rejects \(em
.BR SB5015 .
.TP
.B mapfile
silently ignores.
.TP
.B sash
(the driver) rejects.
.TP
.B sash\-convert
rejects.
.RE
.PP
.B mapfile
is the remaining outlier. The driver used to ignore an unknown flag too \(em
.B sash --bogus script.sh
compiled and exited 0 \(em so a typo such as
.B \-\-emit\-C
silently produced a different artifact than the one requested. Fixed; see
.BR notes/tasks/cli\-usage\-and\-unknown\-flags.md .
.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Every rejection carries an
.B SB
code. Those referenced here:
.RS
.TP
.B SB5011
command resolution failed (e.g.
.BR readarray ).
.TP
.B SB5015
.BR read :
unknown flag.
.TP
.B SB5016
.BR read \ \-f
with a single target and no array \(em
.B standalone shape only,
see BUGS.
.TP
.B SB5019
numeric operator in
.RB \[dq] [[\ ]] \[dq];
use
.RB \[dq] ((\ )) \[dq].
.TP
.B SB5020
tainted value used without
.BR attest .
.TP
.B SB5050
variable assigned but never read.
.TP
.B SB5056
read of the don't-care binder
.BR _ .
.RE
.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bash (1),
.BR sash\-convert (1),
.BR sash\-getopt (1)
.PP
